messages.content:
{
 "what_you_observe": [
  "Tuesday, February 2. Seven pm.",
  "Phil said in the lobby: You do not say anything to Rita or Larry. You nod at them — the small, tired nod of a man who has made up his mind and does not need them to follow. You set the coffee down. You walk. The ballroom door clicks shut behind you.",
  "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
  "Larry said in the lobby: To the crew, over my shoulder, not turning around: \"Taking the charge. Alma's got a girl in Pittsburgh and the place is going dark. You guys shoot the ballroom tonight, I'll be back.\"",
  "Larry records Alma on his last charged take. The reel stays with him.",
  "You are now at Rialto.",
  "There is time for one evening visit. The ballroom lights are on and the station line is open."
 ],
 "your_own_memories": [
  {
   "what_you_did": "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
   "what_you_said": "I don't speak to the crew. I push the door and go.",
   "your_intention": "Record Alma at the Rialto before closing. Her voice, for her daughter in Pittsburgh. My piece. One morning, one room, one woman who has been doing this work for forty-two winters and is about to lose the room she did it in."
  }
 ],
 "who_you_are_with": "your present location",
 "physical_choices": {
  "transmit_interview": "Take the regional line at the station and send any usable interview with Alma to her daughter.",
  "go_dancing": "Spend the evening at the ballroom, open to dancing with a colleague.",
  "go_to_party_for_work": "Cover the party with your notebook, without dancing.",
  "take_evening_off": "Choose a quiet evening on your own."
 }
}

{
  "action": "transmit_interview",
  "detail": "I walk from the booth to the station line. The switchboard is warm, the handset heavy. I take the recording off the patch — my tape, the one where Alma's hands find the reel without looking, where she says *forty-two winters, and the last one's the one that counts* — and I seat it in the player. I dial the Pittsburgh number Phil left taped to the glass. The line clicks. A woman answers, tired, saying *yes, hello, who is this*. I say: *Rita Hanson. This is for your mother. She asked me to send it to you tonight, before the lights go out here. I'm playing it now.* I push the play button. I do not hang up. I sit in the chair and I let Alma's voice go down the wire into a kitchen in Pittsburgh that I will never see, while the ballroom hums its last low electric hum behind me and the projector in the back room ticks, ticks, ticks, counting down its last reel for the last time. I listen to the whole thing. I do not interrupt. When it ends I say *that's all, Ma's voice, forty-two winters, good night* and I set the handset back. I leave the line open. I leave the ballroom lights on. I walk out through the lobby and I do not turn back to look at the Rialto sign. It is dark outside. I walk to the parking lot. I do not get in the car. I stand there a while in the cold, in the quiet, in the dark that is not yet total, and I let the evening be over.","intention": "Get Alma's voice to her daughter tonight. Not tomorrow. Not after the station logs it, files it, decides if it's usable for broadcast. Tonight, on the open line, from this room, while the lights are still on and the projector still ticks. One woman's voice, forty-two winters, to the one person she promised it to. That is the whole job. That is the piece. I am not covering a festival. I am not dancing. I am not making a show. I am sending a mother's voice to her daughter before the room goes dark and the line goes cold and it's just a tape in a drawer somewhere. That is the act. The rest — the nod Phil gave, the crew shooting the ballroom, Larry's last charged reel in his bag — that is the world moving around it. My part is the wire, the click, the voice going out. Done.","words": "Rita Hanson. This is for your mother. She asked me to send it to you tonight, before the lights go out here. I'm playing it now. … That's all. Ma's voice. Forty-two winters. Good night."
}
